    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Smiling figures have long been popular in modern antiquities markets due to their pleasing demeanor, relatively large scale, and the ability of looters to recover large quantities of them. The heads are sometimes mold-made—as is the head displayed here without a body—which enables the relatively easy production of fakes. Few heads have been found in their original contexts, although some were placed in tombs. The young appearance of many heads may reflect a tradition of depicting deceased souls as a combination of wise (the deceased) and youthful (the progeny to come), extending back to the Olmec in the Early Formative period.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation - Group chat 2007-134 and 2016-1075"
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tDecember 1, 1956, Carlebach Gallery, New York (no. 10475-3) sold to Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2016, bequest of Gillett G. Griffin to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] According to a Carlebach Gallery invoice in the curatorial file and matched to the number painted on the object. Information supported by Griffin index card (M18).\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        }
